WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.470 --> 00:00:04.569
This episode of Real Engineering is brought
to you by Brilliant, a problem solving website

00:00:04.569 --> 00:00:07.580
that teaches you to think like an engineer.

00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:12.520
Over the last 100 years, advancements in science
and technology have allowed us to learn so

00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:16.660
much about where we came from and how our
planet has developed over time.

00:00:16.660 --> 00:00:20.980
With our discoveries of old fossils, we know
that life on Earth has existed for at least

00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:22.810
3.5 billion years.

00:00:22.810 --> 00:00:27.830
But with the Earth itself believed to have
formed about 4.5 billion years ago, we still

00:00:27.830 --> 00:00:33.020
have very limited knowledge on what caused
life to form in the first place, and much

00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:37.390
of the evidence on how it may have developed
has actually been destroyed by the very life

00:00:37.390 --> 00:00:38.390
that it created.

00:00:38.390 --> 00:00:43.120
Sadly, we don’t have a time machine, but
we can look for places in our solar system

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:44.620
that emulate early earth.

00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:48.550
That’s where Saturn’s largest Moon ‘Titan’
comes into the picture.

00:00:48.550 --> 00:00:52.990
Although it’s about half the size of Earth,
Titan has the characteristics that we think

00:00:52.990 --> 00:00:56.000
are very similar to Earth in its early days.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:00.810
Titan has a thick atmosphere at around 4.4
times denser than Earth's, and is the only

00:01:00.810 --> 00:01:05.010
Moon in the solar system to have any noticeable
atmosphere at all[1].

00:01:05.010 --> 00:01:09.650
Titan also has large pools of liquid which
follow a similar cycle to the rivers and seas

00:01:09.650 --> 00:01:11.050
we have here on Earth.

00:01:11.050 --> 00:01:16.080
But, instead of water, Titan has seas of methane
which evaporate into clouds, causing it to

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:17.730
rain liquid methane.

00:01:17.730 --> 00:01:21.850
In terms of scientific information, Titan
is a gold mine for scientists.

00:01:21.850 --> 00:01:27.270
But sadly, it’s extremely difficult to get
to and because it’s entirely covered in

00:01:27.270 --> 00:01:32.299
clouds of methane, it’s nearly impossible
to study the surface from a distance[2].

00:01:32.299 --> 00:01:37.450
In 1997, a collaboration between NASA, the
European Space Agency and the Italian Space

00:01:37.450 --> 00:01:42.380
Agency launched the Cassini space probe on
a 7 year journey to reach Saturn.

00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:47.270
The probe was designed to study the entire
Saturn system including its rings and natural

00:01:47.270 --> 00:01:48.270
satellites.

00:01:48.270 --> 00:01:51.260
But Cassini didn’t make the 7 year journey
on its own.

00:01:51.260 --> 00:01:55.690
Attached to the space probe was a small lander
called Huygens which was hoping to become

00:01:55.690 --> 00:01:58.550
the first spacecraft to land on Titan.

00:01:58.550 --> 00:02:03.210
Several months after entering Saturn’s orbit,
Huygens separated from Cassini and started

00:02:03.210 --> 00:02:05.330
its journey towards Titan[3].

00:02:05.330 --> 00:02:09.729
And began sending back vital details about
Titan’s environment, like the fluid properties

00:02:09.729 --> 00:02:13.180
of the atmosphere and the nature of the moon's
surface.

00:02:13.180 --> 00:02:17.880
As it descended Huygens recorded accelerometer
data, which could be used to deduce properties

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:22.650
like density of the fluid as we knew the aerodynamic
properties of the probe.

00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:26.150
It took temperature and pressure readings
to teach us about the thermodynamic properties

00:02:26.150 --> 00:02:27.150
of the atmosphere.

00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:31.630
[16] After two and a half hours of descending
through the unknown, Huygens successfully

00:02:31.630 --> 00:02:36.960
landed on the surface, making it the furthest
spacecraft landing from Earth ever completed.

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:41.930
Although it was only designed to survive for
about 90 minutes whilst on the surface, Huygens

00:02:41.930 --> 00:02:48.410
successfully recorded and sent back 350 images,
revealing a world eerily similar to ours,

00:02:48.410 --> 00:02:53.240
with sharp hills and valleys, and rivers of
methane cutting their way through the landscape.

00:02:53.240 --> 00:02:54.240
[4].

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:59.060
As our quest to learn more about the origins
of life continues, Nasa’s new mission called

00:02:59.060 --> 00:03:03.950
‘Dragonfly’ will begin its journey to
Titan in 2026, and the work of the Cassini

00:03:03.950 --> 00:03:07.840
and Huygen mission will be vital to its success.

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:12.129
Dragonfly is a mobile lander fitted with 8
large rotors that will help it fly around

00:03:12.129 --> 00:03:13.730
the surface like a drone.

00:03:13.730 --> 00:03:18.970
An incredible difficult engineering challenge,
and the data gained from Huygens will be insanely

00:03:18.970 --> 00:03:21.320
valuable when designing the drone.

00:03:21.320 --> 00:03:26.310
Everything from it’s sensor layout, battery
capacity, energy source and propellers design

00:03:26.310 --> 00:03:30.819
will be dictated by what we learned, and those
are exactly the engineering challenges we

00:03:30.819 --> 00:03:33.510
are going to investigate today.

00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:37.940
Dragonfly will have many of the same scientific
instruments as the curiosity rover.

00:03:37.940 --> 00:03:42.519
It will have skid mounted drill to take soil
samples and run it through a mass spectrometer

00:03:42.519 --> 00:03:44.819
to learn more about the soil composition.

00:03:44.819 --> 00:03:49.150
It will be capable of quickly analysing elemental
compositions at landing sites before landing,

00:03:49.150 --> 00:03:52.030
using a neutron-activated gamma-ray spectrometer.

00:03:52.030 --> 00:03:56.709
This instrument typical needs cryocooling,
but thanks to Titan’s subzero temperatures,

00:03:56.709 --> 00:03:59.120
this instrument can be passively cooled.

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:04.280
It will however need to generate its own neutron’s
rather relying on cosmic rays to generate

00:04:04.280 --> 00:04:07.490
them, as the 0atmosphere blocks too much sunlight.

00:04:07.490 --> 00:04:12.060
When it lands a seismometer will give us information
about quakes and reveal the thickness and

00:04:12.060 --> 00:04:17.590
nature of Titan’s icy crust sitting above
what is thought to be liquid water ocean.

00:04:17.590 --> 00:04:22.030
We think this because Cassini witnessed the
surface shifting in position by 30 kilometers

00:04:22.030 --> 00:04:27.190
in just 2 years, indicating that the crust
is floating on top of some kind of liquid

00:04:27.190 --> 00:04:28.190
layer.

00:04:28.190 --> 00:04:31.850
We can also look forward to incredible photos
of Titan’s surface, just like the photos

00:04:31.850 --> 00:04:33.970
we are currently getting from Mars.

00:04:33.970 --> 00:04:34.970
[5]

00:04:34.970 --> 00:04:39.050
Since the air is thicker on Titan and the
gravity is one sevenths of earth’s, dragonfly

00:04:39.050 --> 00:04:43.360
will be able to achieve more thrust on a planet
that needs less lift.

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:47.130
Drastically reducing energy consumption compared
to earth.

00:04:47.130 --> 00:04:51.510
Yet finding that energy to fly on the surface
of Titan is not easy.

00:04:51.510 --> 00:04:56.500
Due to Titan’s distance from the Sun and
its thick atmosphere, the sunlight on Titan’s

00:04:56.500 --> 00:05:01.990
surface is around 100 times weaker than it
is on Earth, making solar panels impractical.

00:05:01.990 --> 00:05:02.990
[6]

00:05:02.990 --> 00:05:06.640
Thankfully we have a lot of practice in a
different type of energy source through missions

00:05:06.640 --> 00:05:12.060
like the Curiosity Rover, which was powered
by Radioisotope Thermoelectric Generator.

00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:18.000
RTG’s work by converting the heat from the
natural decay of a radioisotope into electricity.

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:22.669
Now this isn’t traditional nuclear energy
like I have mistakenly said in the past.

00:05:22.669 --> 00:05:28.800
[7] The RTG does use radioactive materials
to generate electricity, but not through nuclear

00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:29.800
fission.

00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:33.620
It uses a simple principle called the Seebeck
Effect to generate electricity.

00:05:33.620 --> 00:05:38.010
[8] The seebeck effect essentially allows
us to generate an electric current through

00:05:38.010 --> 00:05:42.570
a heat differential, as charge carriers will
move from hot to cold.

00:05:42.570 --> 00:05:48.150
So if we have a heat source and a way of cooling
we can generate a sustained electric current.

00:05:48.150 --> 00:05:52.460
Thankfully radioactive substances generate
heat as they decay.

00:05:52.460 --> 00:05:56.610
Choosing a suitable radioactive material is
our first challenge.

00:05:56.610 --> 00:06:01.080
With any spacecraft a lightweight compact
design is paramount, but we also need the

00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:05.770
material to have a long half life to ensure
a long lived energy source.

00:06:05.770 --> 00:06:11.880
We also need it to primary produce Alpha waves,
as this form of radiation is most easily converted

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:13.930
to heat in a compact space.

00:06:13.930 --> 00:06:20.600
[8] As a result of these requirements Plutonium-238
(Pu-238), Strontium-90 (Sr-90), and Curium-244

00:06:20.600 --> 00:06:23.000
are the most commonly used fuels.

00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:28.320
Next we need a material which is both a thermal
insulator to maximise our temperature differential,

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:31.450
and an electric conductor to maximise our
current.

00:06:31.450 --> 00:06:34.370
These two material properties are typical
linked.

00:06:34.370 --> 00:06:38.990
Materials like copper are both a good thermal
and electrical conductor, and a material like

00:06:38.990 --> 00:06:42.330
iron is a poor thermal and electrical conductor.

00:06:42.330 --> 00:06:47.990
Using these materials in conjunction can create
a crude thermoelectric generator, but the

00:06:47.990 --> 00:06:50.050
efficiency is very low.

00:06:50.050 --> 00:06:54.480
If we can create a material with the best
of both properties, then we can achieve a

00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:56.400
higher efficiency.

00:06:56.400 --> 00:07:01.840
Leading to the use of materials like lead
telluride and tags, which is an alloy of Tellurium

00:07:01.840 --> 00:07:04.240
(Te), Silver (Ag), Germanium (Ge) and Antimony
(Sb).

00:07:04.240 --> 00:07:05.240
[9]

00:07:05.240 --> 00:07:09.880
The thermal electric generator used for the
curiosity rover could generate 110 Watts of

00:07:09.880 --> 00:07:15.740
electrical power.[10] But we will lose some
power generation capability during Dragonfly’s

00:07:15.740 --> 00:07:21.380
8 year journey to Titan, as we cannot turn
a radioactive element on and off on demand

00:07:21.380 --> 00:07:23.039
to conserve energy.

00:07:23.039 --> 00:07:27.479
In fact Dragonfly’s cruise vehicle will
need to be equipped with radiators to bleed

00:07:27.479 --> 00:07:32.670
that heat energy into space to prevent overheating,
just as the Curiosity rover did.

00:07:32.670 --> 00:07:37.190
We will also lose energy to keeping the craft
at operating temperature, as the surface of

00:07:37.190 --> 00:07:44.449
Titan can reach temperatures as low as -180°C,
[11]and to keep some vital systems and scientific

00:07:44.449 --> 00:07:49.480
experiments running, leaving us with about
75 watts to charge while on the ground in

00:07:49.480 --> 00:07:51.490
a best case scenario.

00:07:51.490 --> 00:07:56.789
All of our activities will occur during Titan’s
daylight hours, so we will be aiming to charge

00:07:56.789 --> 00:08:03.199
our batteries during Titan’s nights, which
lasts 192 hours, the same as their daylight

00:08:03.199 --> 00:08:04.199
hours.

00:08:04.199 --> 00:08:09.290
So, it makes sense to make our battery charge
fully in those 192 hours.

00:08:09.290 --> 00:08:12.060
Giving us a 14 kWh battery.

00:08:12.060 --> 00:08:16.990
For comparison, a typical tesla battery is
about 75 kWhs.

00:08:16.990 --> 00:08:23.960
With a specific energy of 100 Wh/kg, that
will make our battery 140 kilograms.

00:08:23.960 --> 00:08:30.380
In practice a smaller battery will probably
be used, and even a 30 kilogram 3 kWh battery

00:08:30.380 --> 00:08:37.529
would provide up to 2 hours of flight time
at 10 m/s, providing a massive 72 kilometre

00:08:37.529 --> 00:08:38.529
range.

00:08:38.529 --> 00:08:43.849
Even more incredible when you consider the
Curiosity Rover has only driven 21 kilometres

00:08:43.849 --> 00:08:47.410
over the past 7 years of its time on Mars.

00:08:47.410 --> 00:08:48.410
[12]

00:08:48.410 --> 00:08:52.490
Ofcourse, Dragonfly won’t fly it’s maximum
range in one hop and will likely take shorter

00:08:52.490 --> 00:08:58.089
safer hops between interesting points during
Titan’s day and one of the most impressive

00:08:58.089 --> 00:09:03.759
things about this mission is how the spacecraft
will navigate its way around the surface.

00:09:03.759 --> 00:09:08.589
Since Titan is so far from Earth, just sending
basic information to Earth and from Earth

00:09:08.589 --> 00:09:09.990
will be difficult.

00:09:09.990 --> 00:09:15.459
The energy requirements to transmit data rises
dramatically as a result of the inverse square

00:09:15.459 --> 00:09:16.459
law.

00:09:16.459 --> 00:09:20.589
[13] The Huygen probe had the advantage of
being able to relay information to the Cassini

00:09:20.589 --> 00:09:24.329
space probe, which had a larger antenna and
higher power.

00:09:24.329 --> 00:09:30.089
Unfortunately for the Dragonfly mission, Cassini
is no longer in orbit around Saturn falling

00:09:30.089 --> 00:09:33.230
into Saturn's atmosphere in 2017.

00:09:33.230 --> 00:09:38.240
[14]So Dragonfly will need to dedicate both
precious power and weight to a large high-gain

00:09:38.240 --> 00:09:42.600
antenna in order to communicate with Earth’s
Deep Space Network.

00:09:42.600 --> 00:09:45.269
On top of the additional energy and weight
requirements,

00:09:45.269 --> 00:09:48.129
Titan B-Roll (E3)
The average round trip communication time

00:09:48.129 --> 00:09:54.879
is around 2 and a half hours, making it impossible
to fly the spacecraft in real time.

00:09:54.879 --> 00:09:59.930
Instead Dragonfly with fly using its own vision,
much like the self flying drones we have here

00:09:59.930 --> 00:10:05.679
on Earth, Dragonfly will use its cameras along
with the onboard gyroscopes and accelerometers

00:10:05.679 --> 00:10:08.540
to travel from one point to another.

00:10:08.540 --> 00:10:12.989
Dragonfly will be trained to identify suitable
landing sites that are flat and free from

00:10:12.989 --> 00:10:15.990
any obstacles like large rocks and rough terrain.

00:10:15.990 --> 00:10:16.990
[15]

00:10:16.990 --> 00:10:20.879
Originally, Dragonfly was intended to fly
with a single rotor, but since helicopters

00:10:20.879 --> 00:10:25.600
are mechanically complex in the way they vary
the rotor’s pitch to vary lift, the idea

00:10:25.600 --> 00:10:26.839
was never developed.

00:10:26.839 --> 00:10:31.680
[15]But with the surge in multi-rotor drone
technology over the last decade, the idea

00:10:31.680 --> 00:10:34.730
for a quadcopter became much more feasible.

00:10:34.730 --> 00:10:40.019
Dragonfly will feature a total of 8 rotors
mounted in pairs in a quadcopter layout.

00:10:40.019 --> 00:10:44.739
Unlike a helicopter rotor—which is designed
to spin at a constant rate—the speed of

00:10:44.739 --> 00:10:49.910
each rotor can been throttled electrically
to vary the amount of lift generated.

00:10:49.910 --> 00:10:54.180
Although its less efficient to have rotors
in this over-under configuration compared

00:10:54.180 --> 00:11:00.360
to a regular quadcopter, it does provide additional
lift while giving some redundancy, as the

00:11:00.360 --> 00:11:05.930
aircraft will be able to achieve stable flight
even with the loss of one motor or rotor.

00:11:05.930 --> 00:11:06.930
[15].

00:11:06.930 --> 00:11:10.899
Since Titan’s atmosphere is made up of mostly
Nitrogen and is much colder than Earth’s

00:11:10.899 --> 00:11:13.769
atmosphere, the viscosity is also much lower.

00:11:13.769 --> 00:11:18.529
Along with the higher density, this means
that the rotors on Dragonfly will be operating

00:11:18.529 --> 00:11:23.279
in a fluid with a much higher Reynolds number
than they would if they were operating on

00:11:23.279 --> 00:11:24.680
Earth.

00:11:24.680 --> 00:11:28.850
Reynolds number is essentially a quantity
that informs engineers whether laminar or

00:11:28.850 --> 00:11:30.589
turbulent flow will develop.

00:11:30.589 --> 00:11:35.170
It’s a little confusing as the number is
not constant for every situation and depends

00:11:35.170 --> 00:11:40.230
largely on the application, but in general
it can be described by this equation for flow

00:11:40.230 --> 00:11:41.410
in a pipe.

00:11:41.410 --> 00:11:45.689
Where inertial forces, trying to keep the
fluid flowing, are the numerators, and viscous

00:11:45.689 --> 00:11:49.470
forces, trying to slow down the fluid, are
the denominator.

00:11:49.470 --> 00:11:53.779
Here a higher density will increase reynolds
number and thus increase the likelihood of

00:11:53.779 --> 00:11:57.839
turbulent flow, and a lower viscosity will
also increase it.

00:11:57.839 --> 00:11:58.839
[22]

00:11:58.839 --> 00:12:01.240
Exactly the scenario we are encountering on
Titan.

00:12:01.240 --> 00:12:05.879
As a result, the propellers had to be designed
differently to work as efficiently as possible

00:12:05.879 --> 00:12:06.879
on Titan.

00:12:06.879 --> 00:12:12.319
They are in fact much closer in design to
wind turbine blades than normal propellers,

00:12:12.319 --> 00:12:14.899
with a large amount of twist in the aerofoil.

00:12:14.899 --> 00:12:19.490
These propellers are a metre in diameter,
much smaller than any wind turbine blade here

00:12:19.490 --> 00:12:25.920
on earth, but as a result of that higher reynolds
number the same design principles are applicable.

00:12:25.920 --> 00:12:30.350
Wind turbines are also designed to be resistant
to a build up of surface dirt, which will

00:12:30.350 --> 00:12:35.879
be a valuable property on another planet with
maintenance crews billions of kilometres away.

00:12:35.879 --> 00:12:41.899
We also need to factor in the lower speed
of sound on Titan, which is about 194 m/s

00:12:41.899 --> 00:12:49.689
versus 340 m/s, so shock wave formation can
occur much sooner at the tips of our propellers.

00:12:49.689 --> 00:12:54.380
This means we have to be mindful of tip speeds
which are determined by prop diameter and

00:12:54.380 --> 00:12:56.079
rotational speed.

00:12:56.079 --> 00:13:02.069
Even with these unique environmental factors
Dragonfly will have a mass of around 450kg

00:13:02.069 --> 00:13:04.540
with fantastic range and max speed.

00:13:04.540 --> 00:13:09.029
Allowing Dragonfly to first land in the sand
dunes of Titan’s Equatorial region before

00:13:09.029 --> 00:13:14.100
eventually making it’s way to Selk Crater,
an impact crater thought to contain all the

00:13:14.100 --> 00:13:18.660
building blocks of life that we are familiar
with here on earth, and may just give us some

00:13:18.660 --> 00:13:21.370
clues about Human’s ultimate question.

00:13:21.370 --> 00:13:23.290
How did we get here.

00:13:23.290 --> 00:13:27.420
You may have noticed that I mentioned the
inverse square law, but did not fully explain

00:13:27.420 --> 00:13:28.420
it.

00:13:28.420 --> 00:13:32.109
This is a law that applies to a huge number
of physical properties and it simply states

00:13:32.109 --> 00:13:36.910
that the intensity of a point source of energy
will decrease with the square of the distance

00:13:36.910 --> 00:13:37.910
away from it.

00:13:37.910 --> 00:13:42.439
[25] This applies to gravity, electric fields
and radiation like the dragonfly trying to

00:13:42.439 --> 00:13:44.610
send radio waves back to earth.

00:13:44.610 --> 00:13:48.939
To learn more about it you could take this
course in gravitational physics to learn how

00:13:48.939 --> 00:13:54.149
Newton deduced that the force of gravity obeys
the inverse-square law, and help you understand

00:13:54.149 --> 00:13:56.870
some of the terms I use in my videos.

